<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Witu Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1858 CE – 1923 CE (approx.) · capital Witu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A refuge state founded on the Kenyan mainland by the deposed rulers of Pate, briefly a German protectorate before passing to Britain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1858 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-simba">Ahmad ibn Fumo Bakari</a>, called Simba, a member of the Nabhani family that had ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pate-sultanate">Pate Sultanate</a>, fled from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a>'s forces to the forests of the mainland and founded a new sultanate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lamu">Lamu</a>. It drew runaway slaves and opponents of Zanzibar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a> was a small, fortified state of farmers and former slaves, ruled by the Nabhani sultan with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> notables. To protect himself from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-simba">Ahmad Simba</a> accepted a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1885, negotiated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denhardt-brothers">Denhardt brothers</a>, and the area was known as German Witu or Wituland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland-Zanzibar Treaty</a> of 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. After Germans were killed there, a British force sacked the town in 1890 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Fumo Bakari fled. Witu was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a>, and the sultanate was formally ended in 1923.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/witu-sultanate.jpg" alt="Witu Sultanate" width="320" height="172"><figcaption>Image: Kipala, vectorised by KCVelaga · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Witu_1890_laut_Voeltzkow.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1858 CE – 1923 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Witu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad Simba, Fumo Bakari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wituland, Sultanate of Witu, Swahililand</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=witu-sultanate" aria-label="Witu Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5803 2267 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Witu Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6123 2523L6140 2512M6123 2523L5300 622M6123 2523L4997 651M6123 2523L6023 2482"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 2512h0M5300 622h0M4997 651h0M6023 2482h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6123 2523h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6123 2523h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Witu. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=witu-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
